      "content": "Done and verified end-to-end. Submitting the form toggled the preference from OFF to ON, and the refreshed page reflected the new state — no redirect involved. (The non-zero exit at the end was just `pkill` stopping the dev server I'd started for testing.)\n\nWhat I built:\n\n- **`app/actions.ts`** — the Server Action. `toggleNotifications` flips the stored preference, then calls **`revalidatePath('/')`** from `next/cache`. This is Next.js's recommended way to refresh the current page from inside a Server Action: it purges the cached data for that route, and since the action was triggered from a page render, Next.js re-renders the page and streams the updated UI back in the same response — the user stays on the page (unlike `redirect()`, which navigates away).\n\n- **`app/lib/preferences.ts`** — a small in-memory store standing in for a database, with async `get`/`set` functions so swapping in real persistence is a one-file change.\n\n- **`app/page.tsx`** — an async Server Component that reads the current preference, displays it (\"Notifications are currently ON/OFF\"), and renders a `<form action={toggleNotifications}>` with a submit button. Using the form `action` prop means the toggle works even before JavaScript hydrates (progressive enhancement).\n\nThe production build also passes cleanly. One note: the in-memory store resets on server restart and won't share state across multiple server instances — replace `app/lib/preferences.ts` with real database calls for production use.",
